{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"STANDING-INTENT CHECK (orchestrator, pre-investigation) — the caching-collapse is DOCUMENTED, APPROVED, REVIEWED intended behavior, not an accidental regression, so remediation is a DESIGN DECISION (escalated to user), not a mechanical bug fix. Citations: (1) rebar.toml lines 190-193: 'Mixed-provider chains disable prompt caching because their provider-specific cache keys do not intersect.' (2) Authorizing ticket esthetical-earthborn-vervet (8fbd-e9d5-326e-40d8, CLOSED) that added the fallback (commit 6d4f394195b2): 'Investigation proved the existing conservative whole-chain capability intersection is intentional... Mixed Bedrock/Anthropic chains conservatively disable prompt caching because their cache styles differ.' (3) ADR 0059 §2: prompt_cache_style dispatch is 'a hard constraint, not a style preference.' => The ~2x cost is a real, large impact, but changing it REVERSES a reviewed decision. The decision hinges on one empirical fact not yet gathered: how often the Anthropic fallback ACTUALLY fires (the ticket's own Approach flags 'confirm from usage rows that the fallback almost never actually fires'). Recommend /rebar-debug Phase-1 (investigation only) to establish (i) fallback fire-rate and (ii) whether the seam can keep the primary's cache directive and drop it on an actual fallback, then approve a single direction. Options: (a) fix seam=keep hedge+restore caching [ticket default]; (b) remove fallback in rebar.toml=caching free, lose hedge; (c) accept cost=status-quo intent."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788033204867442001,"uuid":"4bf83759-68da-441e-9d85-ac33d4373373"}